Present the emulated NVMe controller as an AMD device rather than a Nutanix one.

SPDK hardcodes Nutanix's PCI vendor ID in its vfio-user target, in three
places in lib/nvmf/vfio_user.c: the PCI config header (vendor and subsystem
vendor), and the Identify Controller VID/SSVID. SPDK_PCI_VID_NUTANIX is 0x4e58
-- ASCII "NX" -- and it is there for a historical reason rather than a
technical one: libvfio-user is a Nutanix project, and when SPDK's vfio-user
transport was written they used their own vendor ID rather than squat on a real
NVMe vendor's. There is no RPC or configuration knob for it; nvmf_create_transport
exposes nothing, so the only way to change it is here.

Every other device this repository serves over vfio-user models an AMD part --
rocjitsu a GPU, rocm-ernic a NIC, the SDXI image a data mover -- and this one
is the odd one out. A guest in one of the mixed stacks (qemu-minimal's xio
flavour runs rocjitsu, ernic and this image behind the same guest) sees
4e58:0001 in lspci among AMD devices, and "Nutanix" in `nvme id-ctrl`, with
nothing anywhere to explain it.

Safe to change:

  - The guest's nvme driver binds on PCI class code (01:08:02, set two lines
    below the first hunk and untouched here), not on vendor ID.
  - drivers/nvme/host/pci.c has no quirk entry for 0x1022, so nothing in the
    guest changes behaviour on the new ID. The Nutanix ID has no quirk entry
    either, so this neither gains nor loses one.
  - 1022:0001 is unassigned: AMD's device-ID space in the pci.ids database
    starts at 0x1100, so this collides with no real AMD part. It renders as
    "Advanced Micro Devices, Inc. [AMD] Device 0001".

The IEEE OUI moves with the vendor ID, or the identify data would contradict
itself. 0x8d/0x6b/0x50 is Nutanix's OUI 50:6B:8D in the little-endian byte
order NVMe stores the company ID in; 0x2c/0x27/0x74 is AMD's MA-L assignment
74:27:2C (Advanced Micro Devices, Inc., Austin TX) in the same order.

The device ID stays 0x0001, which is SPDK's own value and carries no meaning.
Changing it as well would invent an AMD part number that does not exist.

Retire this patch if SPDK ever grows a configurable vendor ID -- at which point
this becomes an entrypoint.sh RPC argument rather than a source edit. As with
the other two patches this image carries, `git apply` fails loudly when a
future spdk_commit moves these lines, which is the signal to come back here
rather than to silently build something that no longer matches.

--- a/lib/nvmf/vfio_user.c
+++ b/lib/nvmf/vfio_user.c
@@ -3331,7 +3331,7 @@
 		SPDK_ERRLOG("vfu_ctx %p failed to initialize PCI\n", vfu_ctx);
 		return ret;
 	}
-	vfu_pci_set_id(vfu_ctx, SPDK_PCI_VID_NUTANIX, 0x0001, SPDK_PCI_VID_NUTANIX, 0);
+	vfu_pci_set_id(vfu_ctx, SPDK_PCI_VID_AMD, 0x0001, SPDK_PCI_VID_AMD, 0);
 	/*
 	 * 0x02, controller uses the NVM Express programming interface
 	 * 0x08, non-volatile memory controller
@@ -3802,10 +3802,10 @@
 	vu_transport = SPDK_CONTAINEROF(transport, struct nvmf_vfio_user_transport, transport);
 
-	cdata->vid = SPDK_PCI_VID_NUTANIX;
-	cdata->ssvid = SPDK_PCI_VID_NUTANIX;
-	cdata->ieee[0] = 0x8d;
-	cdata->ieee[1] = 0x6b;
-	cdata->ieee[2] = 0x50;
+	cdata->vid = SPDK_PCI_VID_AMD;
+	cdata->ssvid = SPDK_PCI_VID_AMD;
+	cdata->ieee[0] = 0x2c;
+	cdata->ieee[1] = 0x27;
+	cdata->ieee[2] = 0x74;
 	memset(&cdata->sgls, 0, sizeof(struct spdk_nvme_cdata_sgls));
 	cdata->sgls.supported = SPDK_NVME_SGLS_SUPPORTED_DWORD_ALIGNED;
 	cdata->oncs.nvmcmps = vu_transport->transport.opts.oncs.nvmcmps;
